# Magpie Integrity Verification (Scrub) - Cron Configuration
# Alternative to the systemd timer for systems without systemd.
#
# `magpie-ctl verify` re-reads stored blobs and compares them against the
# SHA-256 recorded at upload time, detecting bit-rot or tampering of artifacts
# (e.g., OpenVPN CA/cert/key material). Nothing else in Magpie re-checks stored
# bytes, so this schedule is the only detection for silent corruption.
#
# Installation options:
#
# 1. Copy to /etc/cron.d/ (recommended):
#    cp magpie-verify /etc/cron.d/
#    chmod 644 /etc/cron.d/magpie-verify
#
# 2. Add to root's crontab:
#    crontab -e
#    # Then paste the command portion only, removing the 'root' user field.
#
# Exit codes: 0 clean, 1 operational error, 3 missing blob/metadata,
# 5 content mismatch, 75 the run was skipped because a lock was held (see
# SKIP_CODE below) - the scrub never started, so storage was NOT checked and
# nothing was found wrong. Cron mails the output to the crontab owner and
# records the exit status, so the entries below deliberately do NOT pipe into
# `logger`: in a pipeline the job's status becomes the status of the last
# command and the scrub result is lost. Where syslog delivery is wanted, wrap
# the pipeline in `bash -o pipefail -c` (see the alternative at the bottom of
# this file) so the scrub's status survives. See docs/monitoring.md for alerting.

# Environment variables (adjust paths as needed)
SHELL=/bin/bash
PATH=/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin
MAGPIE_DIR=/opt/magpie

# Host-side lock files used by flock. VERIFY_LOCK prevents overlapping scrubs;
# GC_LOCK keeps a scrub from running while GC deletes blobs. Take both: GC
# unlinks a blob before its metadata sidecar, so a scrub racing GC can report a
# blob as missing that was in fact collected.
#
# The GC lock is taken with a bounded wait (GC_WAIT seconds) rather than
# --nonblock, so an overrunning nightly collection delays the scrub instead of
# cancelling it. Both locks use `-E $SKIP_CODE` so a run that never started is
# reported as 75 and not as exit 1, which the exit-code table reserves for
# "storage is broken". Alert on 75 only when it repeats: one skipped run means
# GC (or a previous scrub) was still working, while a run of them means the
# schedule never gets to verify anything.
VERIFY_LOCK=/var/run/magpie-verify.lock
GC_LOCK=/var/run/magpie-gc.lock
GC_WAIT=3600
SKIP_CODE=75

# Weekly full scrub, Sunday at 4:00 AM (after the nightly GC run).
# A full scrub re-reads every byte in storage, so keep it off-peak.
# Format: minute hour day month weekday user command
0 4 * * 0 root flock -n -E $SKIP_CODE $VERIFY_LOCK flock -w $GC_WAIT -E $SKIP_CODE $GC_LOCK docker compose -f $MAGPIE_DIR/docker-compose.yml run --rm -T magpie magpie-ctl verify --quiet

# Daily scrub of competition-critical crypto material only. Scoped with --path,
# so it is cheap enough to run every day even on a large store.
0 3 * * * root flock -n -E $SKIP_CODE $VERIFY_LOCK flock -w $GC_WAIT -E $SKIP_CODE $GC_LOCK docker compose -f $MAGPIE_DIR/docker-compose.yml run --rm -T magpie magpie-ctl verify --path openvpn --quiet

# Alternative: bound each run with --max-bytes. NOTE: the walk always starts at
# the beginning of the store and keeps no cursor, so a bounded run repeatedly
# verifies the same head of the store and never advances. Use it only to cap the
# cost of a smoke check; to cover everything, scope separate entries with --path
# or schedule an unbounded full scrub off-peak.
# 0 4 * * * root flock -n -E $SKIP_CODE $VERIFY_LOCK flock -w $GC_WAIT -E $SKIP_CODE $GC_LOCK docker compose -f $MAGPIE_DIR/docker-compose.yml run --rm -T magpie magpie-ctl verify --max-bytes 10737418240 --quiet

# Alternative: Use `exec` into the running magpie container (requires container to be running)
# 0 4 * * 0 root flock -n -E $SKIP_CODE $VERIFY_LOCK flock -w $GC_WAIT -E $SKIP_CODE $GC_LOCK docker compose -f $MAGPIE_DIR/docker-compose.yml exec -T magpie magpie-ctl verify --quiet

# Alternative: Direct execution (if not using Docker)
# 0 4 * * 0 root flock -n -E $SKIP_CODE $VERIFY_LOCK flock -w $GC_WAIT -E $SKIP_CODE $GC_LOCK /usr/local/bin/magpie-ctl verify --quiet

# Alternative: also log to syslog. `bash -o pipefail -c` keeps the job's exit
# status equal to the scrub's, which a plain `... | logger` would discard.
# 0 4 * * 0 root bash -o pipefail -c 'flock -n -E $SKIP_CODE $VERIFY_LOCK flock -w $GC_WAIT -E $SKIP_CODE $GC_LOCK /usr/local/bin/magpie-ctl verify --quiet 2>&1 | logger -t magpie-verify'

# Alternative: machine-readable output for a monitoring scraper.
# 0 4 * * 0 root flock -n -E $SKIP_CODE $VERIFY_LOCK flock -w $GC_WAIT -E $SKIP_CODE $GC_LOCK /usr/local/bin/magpie-ctl --format json verify > /var/lib/magpie/verify.json
# NOTE: a skipped run (75) writes no JSON, so a scraper reading this file must
# treat a stale mtime as "not verified recently" rather than as a clean result.
